Chapter: FsDenyRuntime
  cites Foreword chapter FileSystem

 The filesystem denial branch has a negative test.

 The direction bit is checked at runtime by the compiler's default
 FileSystem servicer (emit-fs-write-servicer-helper, X86_64Helpers): it
 tests cap-filesystem-write before it elevates, and jumps past the whole
 elevate-call-lower sequence when the bit is clear. Until now that jump
 was inspection-verified only. codex/test/fs-servicer exercises the pass
 path and nothing exercised the refusal, because the obvious way to reach
 it cannot be written: a program declaring [FileSystem.Read] that calls
 write-file is rejected at compile time, so the source that would reach
 the branch never becomes a binary.

 The way in is to hold the row and lose the bit. This program declares
 both directions, so it compiles and boots with both granted -- and then
 drops cap-filesystem-write out of its own process-table entry with
 process-restrict-cap, which is what [Capability] is for. The second
 write is made by the same code, against the same disk, one bit apart
 from the first.

 Reporting both writes is the discipline this battery already runs on. A
 test that showed only the refusal would print the same thing whether the
 bit was honoured or writes were broken outright, and a failure for the
 wrong reason reads exactly like a pass:

   granted write True  / denied write False   the bit was honoured. The
                       only passing shape, and it needs both halves.
   granted write False                        writes do not work here at
                       all; the refusal below proves nothing.
   denied write True   the branch is not taken and a program writes with
                       an authority it no longer holds. This is what the
                       test exists to catch.

 The last line is the one that makes the refusal real rather than merely
 reported. A servicer that returned False and elevated anyway would print
 the same "denied write False" and leave the bytes on the disk. Only
 reading back distinguishes a write that was refused from a write that
 landed and lied about it.

 That read is of a SECOND file, and the reason is a trap this test fell
 into first. The denied write originally overwrote OK.TXT and the check
 was that it still said "hello". It does -- but it says "hello" with the
 write bit intact as well, because fat16-write-file answers True to a
 rewrite of an existing name without changing its contents. The check
 could not fail and would have shipped reading as proof. Writing OK2.TXT
 makes absence the evidence: a refused write leaves nothing to read, and
 an admitted one leaves "second" behind. Both are in scope under the "OK"
 prefix, so the grant is not what separates them.

 The read after the drop is the second control: the restrict has to be
 surgical. FileSystem.Read is a separate bit and was never dropped, so a
 machine that answers the read is attenuating the grant, and one that
 refuses it revoked the family. Denying what was legitimately asked for
 is not enforcement.

 No Device.Block anywhere, on purpose. The servicer's elevation is what
 reaches the disk, so this also holds the fs-servicer guarantee steady
 across the drop: the elevation is gated by the direction bit and not a
 door beside it.

Section: Bits

  fsd-cap-bit : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  fsd-cap-bit (word) (n) = if bit-and word (bit-shl 1 n) /= 0 then 1 else 0

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console, Capability, FileSystem.Read "OK", FileSystem.Write "OK"] Nothing
  opening = act
    first <- write-file "OK.TXT" "hello"
    print-line-uni ("granted write " & show first)

    back <- read-text "OK.TXT"
    print-line-uni ("granted read " & back)

    print-line-uni ("write bit " & show (fsd-cap-bit (process-get-cap (process-get-pid)) 7))

    dropped <- process-restrict-cap (process-get-pid) 7
    print-line-uni ("restrict " & show dropped)

    print-line-uni ("write bit " & show (fsd-cap-bit (process-get-cap (process-get-pid)) 7))

    second <- write-file "OK2.TXT" "second"
    print-line-uni ("denied write " & show second)

    after <- read-text "OK2.TXT"
    print-line-uni ("read after [" & after & "]")
  end
